<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Mysore State</h1><p class="st-meta">1799 CE – 1950 CE · capital Mysore</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Wodeyar kingdom restored by the British after Tipu Sultan's fall, later celebrated as a model princely state for its dams, universities and electricity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> in 1799, the British restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> had sidelined, and placed the five-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-iii">Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</a> on the throne under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnaiah">Purnaiah</a>. The new state was smaller than Tipu's realm and bound by a subsidiary alliance.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After a revolt in 1830–31 the British took over direct administration for fifty years, restoring the dynasty in 1881. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-iv">Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV</a> (ruled 1894–1940, with full powers from 1902) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/m-visvesvaraya">M. Visvesvaraya</a>, Mysore built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivanasamudra">Shivanasamudra</a> hydroelectric plant, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-raja-sagara">Krishna Raja Sagara</a> dam, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-mysore">University of Mysore</a> and industries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, which also hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-institute-of-science">Indian Institute of Science</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> culture and Carnatic music flourished at court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachamarajendra-wodeyar">Jayachamarajendra Wodeyar</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> when Mysore became a state of the Indian Union in 1950. It was enlarged in 1956 and renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in 1973.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mysore-state.jpg" alt="Mysore State" width="320" height="387"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mysore_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1799 CE – 1950 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mysore</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Krishnaraja Wodeyar III, Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV, Jayachamarajendra Wodeyar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kannada</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Princely State of Mysore, Mysore Kingdom (1799-1950)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=mysore-state" aria-label="Mysore State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6786 1719 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mysore State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7106 1975L7107 1971M7106 1975L7364 1601M7106 1975L7364 1602M7106 1975L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7107 1971h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7106 1975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7106 1975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=mysore-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
